<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gwent</h1><p class="st-meta">470 CE – 1081 CE (approx.) · capital Caerwent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Welsh kingdom between the Usk and the Wye, heir to the Roman town of Caerwent, which fell to the Normans after 1067.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gwent took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venta-silurum">Venta Silurum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caerwent">Caerwent</a>, the Roman capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/silures">Silures</a>. After Roman rule ended a kingdom formed in the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-usk">Usk</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">Wye</a>, with traditions of a founder named Caradog Freichfras. Its kings were often the same as those of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-glywysing">Kingdom of Glywysing</a>, the two realms passing between branches of one dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-llandaff">Llandaff charters</a> record grants by its kings to churches and show a landscape of royal estates and small monasteries. Brochfael ap Meurig submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred of Wessex</a>, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asser">Asser</a>. The kingdom lay on the English border, and its rulers alternately fought and allied with the Mercians and later the English kings, while the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-usk">Usk</a> valley and coastal levels provided its wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn">Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</a> seized Gwent in the 1050s. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fitzosbern">William FitzOsbern</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chepstow-castle">Chepstow Castle</a> from 1067 and pushed west. Its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caradog-ap-gruffydd">Caradog ap Gruffudd</a>, was killed at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mynydd-carn">Mynydd Carn</a> in 1081, and Gwent became Norman marcher lordships, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-gwent.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Gwent" width="320" height="421"><figcaption>Image: Owj20 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medieval_Wales.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>470 CE – 1081 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caerwent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brochfael ap Meurig, Morgan Hen, Caradog ap Gruffudd</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gwent</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=776&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gwent" aria-label="Kingdom of Gwent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4612 387 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Gwent lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4932 643L4927 648M4932 643L4970 663M4932 643L4943 639"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4927 648h0M4970 663h0M4943 639h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4932 643h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4932 643h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=776&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gwent">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
